 "grid_note": "HOW THE CELLS WERE LOCATED. The 39 vertical rules were detected across the body of the sheet as thin-line maxima of a high-passed darkness profile and fitted as a single pitch, x = 662.5 + 79.35 n — the same pitch printed page 215 carries, which is what a shared plate should give. THE TEMPLATE WAS THEN CHECKED AGAINST THE PRINTED HEADING BEFORE ANY CELL WAS READ, because that check is what catches the one-column slip that ages every mark: rendered over the heading it reads column 1 'Under 5' under MALES, column 13 '100 and upwards', column 14 'Under 5' under FEMALES and column 26 '100 and upwards'. The 31 writing lines were fitted off the names-column ink as y = 704 + 75.5 n, marks sitting above each baseline. Columns 5, 6 and 29 — the two that carry the page's weight and the one that does not close — were each read a second time as a montage of their own 31 cells, and that second read is what settled line 16: what the row band showed in column 5 is an INK BLOT and not a tally, and the column's own footing of 62 is what proves it. No cell value here is an OCR output.",
 "cells_state": "partial",
 "cells_note": "Thirty-seven of the thirty-eight columns balance against the figures the enumerator footed on this sheet, including the nineteen that are blank down the page and blank in the footing and are treated as balanced at zero. ONE DOES NOT: free colored males 24 under 36 (column 29) reads 4 — a 2 on line 18, a 1 on line 25 and a 1 on line 29, and the column was montaged and nothing else is in it — against a printed footing of two identical hooked diagonals, the form this hand foots column 1 with, i.e. 11. It is left standing rather than forced: `cells` omits column 29 and `cells_first_pass` carries the whole reading. It is NOT reconciled data and nothing downstream may treat it as such. THE SHAPE OF THIS SHEET IS ITS OWN FINDING and it is not the shape of printed page 215, which is 31 households of mostly single men: this page is BOARDING HOUSES. 195 free white persons stand on 31 lines and FOUR of those lines carry 104 of them — line 18 (S. M. Osterhoudt) 33, line 29 (G. W. Cook) 27, line 10 (Wm R. Miller) 22 and line 17 (Lyman Butterfield) 22 — each a stack in the young male bands with a woman or two beside it. Column 5, free white males 20 under 30, carries 62 of the page's 126 men on its own, and column 18, free white females 20 under 30, carries 30 of its 69 women. Against that, twelve of the 31 households hold two persons or fewer and ten of those hold one or none. The FREE COLORED block is filled on three lines only — 18, 25 and 29 — and line 25 (Oliver Henson) is the one household on the page with NO free white person in it at all: as read it is a boy under 10, a man 24 under 36 and a woman 24 under 36.",
